<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="violaxanthin, antheraxanthin and zeaxanthin interconversion" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-5945" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Arabidopsis thaliana col&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Common Viridiplantae Pathways (CVP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are thought to be present in all or most Viridiplantae species including algae. All UPP pathways are automatically imported into databases regardless of Pathologic predictions. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; |FRAME: carotenoids Carotenoids| are isoprenoid pigments in the yellow to red color range. They have conjugated double bond chains of varying length with varying substituents, which determines their light absorption spectrum. They are produced by photosynthetic organisms and some non-photosynthetic fungi and bacteria. Animals, including humans, cannot synthesize carotenoids, although they are an essential source of retinoids and vitamin A. Carotenoids have diverse functions that include participation in photosynthetic light-harvesting complexes, serving as antioxidants, virulence factors, and modulation of membrane function. In photosynthetic organisms carotenoids provide protection against photooxidative stress through energy-dissipation of excess light absorbed by the antenna pigments as well as light-harvest. Plants and algae are exposed to rapidly changing light conditions and light intensities. Illumination with high light intensities has the potential to overexcite the photosynthetic pigments and the electron transport chain and induce the production of toxic reactive oxygen species. Many photoprotection mechanisms exist to prevent this problem. One of the most important protection mechanisms is the dissipation of excessive excitation energy as heat in the light-harvesting complexes of the photosystems. This process requires a structural change of the photosynthetic antenna complexes that are normally optimized for efficient light-harvesting. Enhanced heat dissipation in the antenna systems is achieved by a strong quenching of the chlorophyll a fluorescence, and has been termed non-photochemical quenching, or NPQ |CITS: [24854581]|. &lt;b&gt;About This Pathway&lt;/b&gt; One of the important factors for the induction of NPQ in higher plants is the operation of the |FRAME: CPD1F-133|, |FRAME: CPD1F-131|, |FRAME: CPD1F-130| (VAZ) cycle. In the forward reaction of the cycle, which takes place under illumination with high light intensities, the di-epoxy xanthophyll |FRAME: CPD1F-133| is de-epoxidized to the epoxy-free xanthophyll |FRAME: CPD1F-130| via |FRAME: CPD1F-131|, which contains one epoxy group. In the reverse reaction, which occurs in low light condition, |FRAME: CPD1F-130| is re-epoxidized to |FRAME: CPD1F-133|. The forward reaction is carried out by |FRAME: EC-1.14.15.21|. The enzyme has a low pH optimum of 5.2 and requires the reduced form of ascorbate as cosubstrate. Prior to activation the enzyme is localized in the thylakoid lumen in an inactive form. After the drop of the lumenal pH to a value of around 5, due to the light-driven electron transport and the build-up of the transmembrane proton gradient, the enzyme forms an active dimer, and binds to the thylakoid membrane. The reverse reaction is catalyzed by |FRAME: EC-1.23.5.1|. The enzyme is located at the stromal side of the thylakoid membrane and has a broad pH optimum. It is usually active at neutral or slightly basic pH values that are typical for the chloroplast stroma. It is active both during darkness and illumination, but since its rate slower by a factor of 10 than the respective de-epoxidation rate, its activity during periods of high light illumination does not inhibit the fast accumulation of |FRAME: CPD1F-130|.</Description>
